/**
 * Commercial vertical — warehouses. C-07 verified.
 *
 * REGULATORY: 4 TAC §7.146(c) names "warehouse" explicitly. Sign to the chief
 * administrator, IPM Coordinator or building manager >=48h before a planned
 * indoor treatment; §7.148(b) puts the posting duty on them, area of common
 * access, >=48h ahead, plus the consumer information sheet on request.
 * A warehouse is also a WORKPLACE under §7.146(b)/§7.147(d).
 * §7.144 two-year use records; §7.145 invoice/contract; §7.123 insurance.
 *
 * FUMIGATION IS NOT OFFERED. Structural Fumigation and Commodity Fumigation
 * are separate categories under §7.124 and neither is held. Commodity storage
 * operations that need fumigation must be told to go elsewhere. This is the
 * hardest honesty gate on this page because warehouses are exactly where the
 * request comes from.
 *
 * RODENT FACTS, all sourced:
 *  - House mouse: gap 1/4 in (AgriLife School IPM: "as small as 1/4 inch in
 *    diameter", "a hole the size of a pencil diameter"; UC IPM: under 1/4 in
 *    tall, through 3/8 in wide). Rats: slightly more than 1/2 in; UC IPM says
 *    seal everything over 1/4 in because juveniles are the concern.
 *  - Mouse: 5-10 litters/yr of 5-6, gestation 18-21 days, mature 5-6 weeks.
 *    Eats ~20% body weight daily in ~200 small meals nightly -> many small
 *    placements beat few large. Home range 10-30 ft, seldom >30 ft from nest,
 *    returns up to 40x/day. Traps <=10 ft apart, close to walls.
 *  - Thigmotaxis: rodents travel linear pathways, wall/floor corners, base of
 *    foundations (AgriLife School IPM).
 *  - Mice are NOT neophobic - "quickly detect new objects but do not fear
 *    them", enter bait stations almost immediately. RATS ARE neophobic - both
 *    roof and Norway rats "are leery of new things", requiring pre-baiting.
 *    This is a genuine, sourced technical contrast.
 *  - Roof rat: climbs, nests ABOVE ground (attics, wall/ceiling voids), forages
 *    up to 300 ft. Norway rat: burrows, ground floors, forages ~150 ft
 *    (AgriLife School IPM). Roof rats climb vertical pipes up to 3 in dia.,
 *    jump 36 in vertically / 48 in horizontally; Norway rats burrow >=36 in.
 *  - Rub/smudge marks are a RAT sign, not a mouse sign. Gnaw marks: mouse
 *    ~1/12 in (2mm), rat ~1/6 in (4mm).
 *  - Seal with METAL OR CONCRETE. Foam, plastic, wood, rubber and steel wool
 *    are gnawable (NMSU L-209) - note this conflicts with AgriLife's steel-wool
 *    suggestion for scorpion weep holes; steel wool is fine against arthropods,
 *    NOT as a permanent rodent seal. 24-gauge 1/4x1/4 hardware cloth for mice,
 *    19-gauge 1/2x1/2 for rats, galvanised sheet 24 gauge or heavier. Never
 *    lightweight chicken wire.
 *  - Store materials 18 in off the floor with space from walls (NMSU).
 *  ⚠️ Do NOT assert roof vs Norway prevalence for this region - no source.
 *    Both occur in Texas towns; identify by evidence on site.
 *  ⚠️ HANTAVIRUS is carried by the DEER MOUSE (Peromyscus), NOT house mice or
 *    commensal rats. Do not blur them. Never sweep or vacuum droppings.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
