<BaseLayout
  title="Warehouse Pest Control | Permian Basin"
  description="Rodent programmes, structural exclusion and documented monitoring for warehouses and storage across West Texas. No fumigation — and we say so up front."
  path="/commercial/warehouses/"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <Crumbs items={crumbs} />

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Commercial · warehouses</p>
      <h1>Warehouses and storage</h1>
      <div class="measure"><AnswerBox text={quickAnswer} /></div>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Start with what we cannot do, because warehouses are exactly where the question comes up and it
          is better answered in the first minute than the third week.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>We do not fumigate.</strong> Structural fumigation and commodity fumigation are two
          separate licence categories under 4 TAC §7.124, and neither is held on this licence. If your
          operation requires fumigation of the structure or of stored commodity, you need a company
          certified for it. That is a straight answer and you will get it on the phone.
        </p>
        <p>
          What is offered is rodent programmes, structural exclusion, general pest work, monitoring, and
          documentation good enough to satisfy whoever audits you. On most warehouses in this region that
          is the whole requirement.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The threshold under the roll-up door</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          If there is one thing to take from this page, it is that most warehouse rodent problems are a
          door problem, and the door in question works perfectly.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A roll-up door that no longer seats along its whole width</strong> is an open invitation
          and it is invisible from the inside, because the door still closes and still latches. The gap is
          usually at the ends, or where the slab has settled, or where a strip has been damaged by a
          forklift and never replaced.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The dimension that matters is a quarter of an inch.</strong> AgriLife's guidance
          describes mice getting through openings as small as a quarter inch in diameter — a hole the size
          of a pencil. Rats need slightly more than half an inch. Extension exclusion standards say seal
          everything over a quarter inch regardless, because juveniles are the concern and because you
          rarely know at the outset which species you are dealing with.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Then everything else on the envelope.</strong> Personnel doors and their sweeps. Dock
          levellers and the void behind them, which is one of the most productive harbourage points in any
          warehouse and almost never inspected. Utility penetrations for water, electrical, gas, air lines
          and data. Vents and their screens. Where the wall panel meets the slab. Anywhere a contractor cut
          an opening and did not close it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And the doors that stand open all day.</strong> In this climate, and in this industry,
          that is normal operating practice and it is not going to change. What can change is what is
          immediately outside them — vegetation, stored pallets, accumulated material — because an open
          door twenty feet from good harbourage is a very different proposition from an open door with
          clear ground around it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Seal with metal or concrete.</strong> Foam, plastic, wood and rubber are gnawable and
          should not be relied on as a permanent rodent seal — New Mexico State extension is explicit about
          it, and steel wool belongs on that list for rodent work too, even though it is perfectly adequate
          against insects. Quarter-inch hardware cloth at 24 gauge for mice, half-inch mesh at 19 gauge for
          rats, galvanised sheet at 24 gauge or heavier. Never lightweight chicken wire.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>How rodents actually move through a building</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Understanding this changes where devices go, and it is the difference between a programme that
          works and one that generates paperwork.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>They travel along edges.</strong> The behaviour has a name — thigmotaxis — and AgriLife
          names it directly: rodents travel linear pathways, the corners between walls and floors, and the
          base of foundations. They do not cross open floor if they can help it. In a warehouse that means
          the perimeter wall, the base of racking, the line where pallets meet the wall, and any run of
          conduit or pipe at low level.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Mice work in a very small radius.</strong> A house mouse seldom ventures more than about
          thirty feet from its nest and food supply, occupying an area roughly ten to thirty feet across
          and returning to the nest up to forty times a day. That is the operational reason mouse control
          is about density of devices in a small area rather than perimeter coverage of a large one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And they feed in tiny amounts, constantly.</strong> Up to twenty percent of body weight
          daily, taken in roughly two hundred small meals a night. Which is why many small bait and trap
          placements beat a few large ones, and why UC IPM's guidance is traps no more than about ten feet
          apart in an active area, set close to the wall so the animal passes directly over the trigger.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Rats range much further.</strong> AgriLife's figures are that roof rats travel up to
          three hundred feet in search of food and Norway rats approximately a hundred and fifty. So a rat
          problem in your building may be sourced well outside it, and the perimeter and the neighbours
          matter in a way they do not for mice.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The two rat species behave differently and nest differently.</strong> Roof rats are agile
          climbers that nest above ground — attics, wall voids, ceiling voids, and outdoors in trees and
          shrubs — and they can climb vertical pipes up to three inches in diameter and jump thirty-six
          inches vertically. Norway rats are burrowers, nesting along building foundations and beneath
          rubbish or woodpiles, occupying ground floors, and burrowing at least thirty-six inches straight
          down. Both occur in Texas towns, and rather than assert which dominates here — no source gives
          that for this region — we identify by the evidence on site.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Neophobia, and why rat work starts slowly</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          This is the single most useful piece of technical knowledge for anybody who has tried to handle
          a rodent problem in-house and concluded that traps do not work.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Mice are not neophobic.</strong> They quickly detect new objects in their environment but
          do not fear them, and will enter a bait station and sample bait almost immediately. Mouse work is
          therefore fast: put out enough devices, correctly placed, and you get a result within days.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Rats are the opposite.</strong> AgriLife's own material states that both roof and Norway
          rats are leery of new things in their environment. A trap placed and set on day one is a trap
          that rats will avoid, sometimes for a week or more, and the operator concludes there are no rats
          — or worse, that the population is bait-shy.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Which is why rat work is pre-baited.</strong> Devices go out unset, baited, and left
          until the animals are feeding on them confidently. Then they are set, all at once. That sequence
          feels like nothing is happening and it is the reason the job works.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And the most common in-house mistake, per AgriLife's field advice:</strong> not putting
          out enough traps. A handful of devices against an established population extends the job
          indefinitely and educates the survivors. The correct approach is enough devices to make the
          trapping period short and decisive.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Reading the evidence.</strong> Mouse gnaw marks run about a twelfth of an inch — two
          millimetres. Rat gnaw marks about a sixth of an inch — four millimetres. Smudge or rub marks
          along beams, rafters, pipes and walls, caused by rats rubbing their fur against surfaces on
          habitual runways, are a rat sign and not a mouse sign. Droppings differ in size and in whether
          the ends are pointed. A long-standing mouse infestation carries a characteristic musky odour.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Reproduction is why a small problem does not stay small.</strong> A house mouse produces
          five to ten litters a year of five or six young, with a gestation of eighteen to twenty-one days
          and sexual maturity at five to six weeks. Those numbers compound quickly enough that a
          fortnight's delay in a warehouse is a materially larger job, and they are the reason the correct
          response to first evidence is a decisive one rather than a watching brief.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Photograph rather than describe.</strong> Droppings and gnaw marks photograph well with
          something beside them for scale, and it settles the species question far faster than any
          conversation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Storage practice does half the work</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          None of this is chemical and all of it makes the treatment side easier and cheaper.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Eighteen inches off the floor, and away from the walls.</strong> New Mexico State
          extension's standard, and it does two things at once: it removes the harbourage under and behind
          stored material, and — just as importantly — it lets you see the runway. You cannot inspect a
          wall base you cannot reach, and a perimeter aisle is worth more than any monitoring device
          placed in front of a pallet.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Food and feed in metal, glass or heavy plastic with tight lids.</strong> Bagged product
          sitting on a pallet is a food source with a delivery date attached, and no control programme
          outcompetes a permanent food supply — that is true in a barn and it is equally true across a
          hundred thousand square feet.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Cardboard is a material problem.</strong> Corrugated board is harbourage, nesting
          material and a common introduction route for stored product insects and German cockroaches
          arriving on incoming freight. Breaking it down promptly rather than accumulating it changes more
          than most people expect.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Rotate stock and inspect the back.</strong> The pallet that has not moved in eight months
          is where the problem is, every time. Long-term static storage in a corner is functionally a
          harbourage installation, and the longer it sits the less likely anybody is to look behind it —
          which is precisely why it is worth putting on a schedule rather than leaving to whenever the
          stock finally moves.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Watch what arrives.</strong> Inbound freight is the main introduction route for a
          warehouse — rodents, stored product insects and cockroaches alike. Inspecting incoming pallets
          at the dock, particularly anything that has been sitting in a yard or transferred through several
          hands, closes off more problems than any treatment resolves afterwards.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And manage the exterior.</strong> Vegetation back from the wall, no stored pallets or
          scrap against the building, and the ground clear where doors open. In this region that also
          matters for scorpions and for field-driven rodent pressure after harvest — a warehouse on the
          edge of town gets a seasonal wave from the surrounding ground at roughly the same point every
          year.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>A word about deer mice</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          This one is worth a section on its own, because it is a genuine rural West Texas consideration
          and because it is routinely blurred with the ordinary house mouse.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Hantavirus is carried by the deer mouse</strong>, <em>Peromyscus maniculatus</em>. The
          CDC identifies it as the host of the most common hantavirus causing pulmonary syndrome in the
          United States. It is <em>not</em> carried by house mice or by commensal rats, and attaching it to
          them — which a good deal of pest control marketing does — is both inaccurate and unhelpful.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Telling them apart.</strong> A house mouse is light brownish to grey overall with a
          relatively hairless tail. A deer mouse has a white belly and a bicoloured, moderately to well
          furred tail. That tail is the practical distinction.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Where it matters here.</strong> Outbuildings, barns, equipment sheds and rural structures
          — including storage buildings on the edge of towns and on agricultural property. Not typically
          an urban warehouse problem, and genuinely worth thinking about on a rural one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The handling rule, which is the important part.</strong> Never sweep or vacuum rodent
          droppings. Transmission is by inhaling aerosolised urine, droppings or saliva, and sweeping is
          precisely the action that aerosolises them. The CDC guidance is to wet the material with a ten
          percent bleach solution — about a cup and a half of bleach per gallon of water — wait an hour,
          and double-bag it, using an N-100 or HEPA respirator rather than a painter's mask.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>If you have a building that has been closed up with rodent activity in it</strong>, that
          is the situation the guidance is written for. Ventilate before entering, and do not start with a
          broom in your hand.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Programme, documentation and notice</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Monitoring devices reported over time</strong>, rather than a fixed application schedule.
          On a warehouse the value of a programme is the trend — which stations are taking, which corner
          went active after a rack was moved, whether last quarter's exclusion held. A programme that
          produces readings can be escalated intelligently; one that produces visits cannot.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A device map that reflects the building</strong>, placed on the runways and at the entry
          points rather than at even intervals because it looks systematic.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Records for two years, as 4 TAC §7.144 requires</strong> — pesticides with EPA
          registration numbers, amounts, mixing rates, devices used and quantities, target pest, dates, and
          the applicator's name and licence number. Available to the Department on written or verbal
          request. If you are audited by a customer, a franchisor or an insurer, this is the file they
          want.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Invoices and contracts</strong> carrying the business name, licence number, telephone and
          address under §7.145, with the jurisdiction statement on contracts.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Certificate of insurance</strong> naming your entity, on request. Warehouses almost
          always require one before a vendor comes on site, so arrange it before the first visit rather
          than at the gate.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And the notice rule applies to you twice.</strong> Warehouses are named explicitly in
          4 TAC §7.146(c), and a warehouse is also a workplace under §7.146(b). We supply the sign at least
          forty-eight hours before a planned indoor treatment; under §7.148(b) the building manager or
          employer posts it in an area of common access at least forty-eight hours ahead, and makes the
          consumer information sheet available to anyone working in the building who asks for it. That
          second duty is yours, and most operations have never been told about it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faqs} heading="Warehouse pest control questions" />
  <Cta
    line="Tell him the floor area, what is stored, and whether the roll-up doors seat properly along their whole width. That last one answers more than it sounds like it should."
  />
</BaseLayout>
